                <h2>Scenario: Monday Morning, the Ad Account Was Suspended</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team's operations leader received an email from Meta Business Support on Monday morning: the ad account was suspended due to "suspicious payment activity." After logging in, they found not only all ads paused but also the top-up balance became inaccessible.</p>
<p>This is a typical ad account risk event. Improper handling could lead to:</p>
<ul>
<li>The week's campaign completely stalled</li>
<li>Top-up balance frozen with no withdrawal</li>
<li>Account reputation damaged affecting future account creation</li>
</ul>
<p>The team's response is worth breaking down and learning from.</p>
<h2>Problem Diagnosis: Four Steps to Locate Root Cause</h2>
<p>When facing account suspension, the first reaction shouldn't be repeated appeals but calmly identifying the problem.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Confirm Suspension Type</h3>
<p>Account suspensions typically fall into two categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment-related suspension</strong>: Related to top-up methods, billing information, credit card risk control</li>
<li><strong>Policy violation suspension</strong>: Related to ad content, landing pages, audience targeting violations</li>
</ul>
<p>The email clearly mentioned "suspicious payment activity," belonging to the former. Next steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check recent large top-ups</li>
<li>Verify if payment methods were frequently changed</li>
<li>Confirm if billing addresses match credit card registration info</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Check Account Quality Score Changes</h3>
<p>In Business Manager, click "Account Quality" to review policy violation records. Recent ad rejections can also trigger associated suspensions.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Review Recent Operation Logs</h3>
<p>Look back at operations in the last 72 hours:</p>
<ul>
<li>Any new ad tools or API integrations used</li>
<li>Sudden large budget adjustments</li>
<li>Abnormal login location changes</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 4: Prepare Appeal Materials</h3>
<p>After confirming the root cause, organize the following for submission:</p>
<ul>
<li>Business license or company documentation</li>
<li>Last three months of ad spending statements</li>
<li>Payment method screenshots and authorization proof</li>
<li>Problem explanation and rectification commitment</li>
</ul>
<h2>Actions: 72-Hour Emergency SOP</h2>
<h3>Within 24 Hours: Stop Bleeding + Evidence Collection</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pause all ad campaigns</strong> to avoid further triggering risk controls</li>
<li><strong>Contact the account manager</strong> (if assigned) to explain and request priority handling</li>
<li><strong>Submit the first appeal</strong> with complete materials, avoiding duplicate submissions</li>
</ol>
<h3>Within 48 Hours: Activate Backup Plan</h3>
<ol>
<li>If the account cannot recover short-term, immediately apply for a <strong>new account</strong> for urgent campaigns</li>
<li>Use <strong>multi-account management</strong> tools to uniformly monitor original and new accounts</li>
<li>Check other platforms (Google Ads, TikTok) account status to prevent chain suspensions</li>
</ol>
<h3>Within 72 Hours: Follow-up + Review</h3>
<ol>
<li>Continuously follow up on appeal progress, check for responses every 24 hours</li>
<li>If the first appeal fails, <strong>adjust the appeal language</strong>, emphasizing compliance rectification measures</li>
<li>Complete incident review and output an "Account Risk Response Manual" for team reference</li>
</ol>
<h2>Results: Account Unblocked in 48 Hours</h2>
<p>The team followed the SOP and provided detailed explanation of the payment anomaly (credit card bank risk control misjudgment) along with the bank's transaction proof in the first appeal. After 48 hours, the account resumed normal operation.</p>
<p>More importantly, the team established a <strong>monthly compliance inspection mechanism</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check account quality scores weekly</li>
<li>Verify top-up records and statement consistency monthly</li>
<li>Conduct policy compliance training for the ad team quarterly</li>
</ul>
<h2>Compliance Inspection Checklist: Prevention Over Cure</h2>
